@page
@model SimulationViewer.Pages.MedeaModel

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>SMS &mdash; MEDEA Element</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/site.css" />
</head>
<body>
    <div class="nav-bar">
        <a href="/" class="btn btn-back">&larr; All models</a>
        <span class="nav-title">MEDEA selfish genetic element</span>
    </div>

    <div class="config-panel">

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Population &amp; Release</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="generations">Generations</label>
                <input type="range" id="generations" min="5" max="100" step="1" value="40" />
                <span class="param-value" id="generations-val">40</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="releaseNumber">Release males</label>
                <input type="range" id="releaseNumber" min="1" max="250" step="1" value="150" />
                <span class="param-value" id="releaseNumber-val">150</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="mortality">Mortality rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="mortality" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.01" value="0.10" />
                <span class="param-value" id="mortality-val">0.10</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Reproduction</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="eggsPerFemale">Eggs per female</label>
                <input type="range" id="eggsPerFemale" min="1" max="200" step="1" value="50" />
                <span class="param-value" id="eggsPerFemale-val">50</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Toxin &amp; Rescue</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="medeaPenetrance">Toxin penetrance</label>
                <input type="range" id="medeaPenetrance" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="1.00" />
                <span class="param-value" id="medeaPenetrance-val">1.00</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="medeaRescue">Rescue efficiency</label>
                <input type="range" id="medeaRescue" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="1.00" />
                <span class="param-value" id="medeaRescue-val">1.00</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="medeaDistance">Toxin&ndash;rescue distance</label>
                <input type="range" id="medeaDistance" min="0" max="0.5" step="0.005" value="0" />
                <span class="param-value" id="medeaDistance-val">0.000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="linkage-detail"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Migration</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="migrationBaseRate">Base migration rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="migrationBaseRate" min="0" max="0.5" step="0.001" value="0.100" />
                <span class="param-value" id="migrationBaseRate-val">0.100</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="migration-detail"></div>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="action-bar">
        <button id="btn-run" class="btn btn-run" onclick="runSimulation()">Run Simulation</button>
        <a id="btn-view" href="/Viewer?model=medea" class="btn btn-view disabled">View Results</a>
    </div>

    <div id="progress-container" class="progress-container" style="display:none">
        <div class="progress-bar">
            <div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="progress-text" id="progress-text"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        var SIM_MODEL = 'medea';
        var SIM_PARAMS = [
            'generations', 'releaseNumber', 'mortality', 'eggsPerFemale',
            'medeaPenetrance', 'medeaRescue', 'medeaDistance', 'migrationBaseRate'
        ];
    </script>
    <script src="/js/configure.js?v=3"></script>

    <div class="info-panel">

        <h2>About MEDEA Elements</h2>

        <p>
            <strong>MEDEA</strong> stands for <em>Maternal-Effect Dominant Embryonic
            Arrest</em>, and the acronym is also a reference to the Medea of Greek myth,
            who killed her own children. The element is a two-part construct: a
            <strong>toxin</strong> expressed in the mother's germline and loaded into
            every egg she lays, paired with a <strong>rescue</strong> expressed in the
            embryo from its own genome. An embryo that inherits the element makes the
            rescue and develops normally. An embryo that does not inherit it receives the
            toxin with no antidote and arrests.
        </p>
        <p>
            The consequence is elegant. A heterozygous mother poisons her whole clutch,
            but only the half of her offspring that failed to inherit the element actually
            die. Among her surviving offspring, essentially all are carriers. The element
            does not copy itself, cut anything, or bias meiosis &mdash; inheritance stays
            perfectly Mendelian. It spreads purely because non-carriers are selectively
            removed from carrier broods, which is enough to take it from a modest release
            frequency all the way to fixation.
        </p>
        <p>
            Crucially, MEDEA is a <strong>population modification</strong> system, not a
            suppression system. Its load falls on embryos, and in a species that lays far
            more eggs than can survive to adulthood, killing embryos costs the population
            almost nothing &mdash; density-dependent competition would have removed most
            of them anyway. Adult numbers stay essentially flat while the element sweeps
            through. That is exactly what makes it interesting: it is a vehicle for
            <em>carrying</em> something else, such as a gene conferring refractoriness to
            <em>Plasmodium</em>, into a wild population, leaving the mosquitoes in place
            but no longer able to transmit malaria.
        </p>
        <p>
            Natural Medea factors were discovered in the flour beetle
            <em>Tribolium castaneum</em> by Beeman et al. (1992,
            <em>Genetics</em>), where they had swept through wild populations on their
            own. Chen et al. (2007, <em>Science</em>) built the first synthetic version in
            <em>Drosophila melanogaster</em>, using a maternally expressed microRNA to
            silence an essential embryonic gene together with a zygotically expressed,
            microRNA-resistant copy of that same gene as the rescue; it drove to high
            frequency in cage populations. Akbari et al. (2014, <em>Current Biology</em>)
            later showed the same design working across multiple <em>Drosophila</em>
            genetic backgrounds. On the theoretical side, Wade &amp; Beeman (1994,
            <em>Genetics</em>) and Ward et al. (2011, <em>Evolution</em>) established the
            threshold conditions for spread, and Marshall &amp; Hay (2012,
            <em>Journal of Heredity</em>) compared Medea against other threshold-dependent
            systems. No synthetic Medea has yet been reported in <em>Anopheles</em>, so the
            configuration here is a projection of the <em>Drosophila</em> design onto a
            mosquito life history.
        </p>

        <h2>Parameter Guide</h2>

        <h3>Generations</h3>
        <p>
            The number of discrete, non-overlapping generations to simulate (5&ndash;100).
            MEDEA spreads more slowly than a homing drive because it gains only the
            offspring that non-carriers lose, so give it room &mdash; 40&ndash;60
            generations is usually needed to see a full sweep to fixation, and longer if
            the release was small.
        </p>

        <h3>Release Males</h3>
        <p>
            The number of MEDEA males released into Population 1 at generation 3
            (range: 1&ndash;250). Each is heterozygous for an intact element. Note that
            releasing <em>males</em> costs one generation of lag: the toxin is a maternal
            effect, so nothing happens until the element has passed into females in the
            following generation. Release size matters more here than for a homing drive,
            because a MEDEA element with any fitness cost has a
            <strong>release threshold</strong> &mdash; below some starting frequency it is
            lost instead of spreading.
        </p>

        <h3>Mortality Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation background mortality (0.1&ndash;0.9), which sets how many
            mate-search attempts each female gets: (1 &minus; mortality) &times; carrying
            capacity. For MEDEA this mostly acts as a general brake on reproductive
            output rather than interacting with the drive mechanism itself.
        </p>

        <h3>Eggs per Female</h3>
        <p>
            Base fecundity per successful mating (1&ndash;200). This determines whether
            MEDEA's embryonic killing is invisible or costly. With a large reproductive
            surplus (50 eggs against a cap of 500) losing half the brood of carrier
            mothers barely dents adult numbers, and the element behaves as a pure
            modification system. Reduce fecundity to 5&ndash;10 and the same killing
            starts to bite: eggs become limiting, and MEDEA begins to suppress the
            population as a side effect. Contrasting a high and a low setting here is the
            clearest way to see why &ldquo;modification&rdquo; and
            &ldquo;suppression&rdquo; are a property of the ecology as much as of the
            construct.
        </p>

        <h3>Toxin Penetrance</h3>
        <p>
            The probability (0&ndash;1) that an embryo which received toxin but no rescue
            actually arrests. At 1.0 every non-carrier offspring of a carrier mother dies,
            which is the maximum selective advantage the element can have. Lower values
            make the toxin <em>leaky</em>: some non-carriers survive, wild-type alleles
            persist, and the sweep slows down or stalls. At 0 the toxin does nothing and
            the element is inherited as an ordinary Mendelian allele, drifting rather than
            driving. Leakiness is the most common way a real synthetic Medea
            underperforms, since maternal expression is rarely complete in every egg.
        </p>

        <h3>Rescue Efficiency</h3>
        <p>
            The probability (0&ndash;1) that an embryo which inherited the rescue survives
            a toxin-loaded egg. At 1.0 the rescue is perfect and carriers pay nothing.
            Below 1.0 some carriers die too, and this is the element's
            <strong>fitness cost</strong> &mdash; the parameter that creates its release
            threshold. An imperfect rescue penalises carriers in exactly the situations
            where the element is common, so a MEDEA element with a weak rescue can spread
            from a large release but be driven out from a small one. Setting rescue well
            below penetrance is a good way to demonstrate threshold behaviour: try a large
            and a small release with rescue at 0.8 and compare.
        </p>

        <h3>Toxin&ndash;Rescue Distance</h3>
        <p>
            The genetic map distance (0&ndash;0.5) between the toxin locus and the rescue
            locus, which sets the recombination frequency between them &mdash; shown under
            the slider. At 0 the two halves are perfectly linked and travel as a single
            element, exactly as in a real construct where toxin and rescue sit in one
            insertion.
        </p>
        <p>
            Increase it and the element becomes self-destroying. A crossover between the
            two loci in a heterozygote produces two new chromosome types:
            <em>toxin-only</em>, whose carriers poison their eggs but have no immunity of
            their own, and <em>rescue-only</em>, which confers immunity while paying
            nothing to make toxin. The rescue-only chromosome is a <strong>free
            rider</strong>: it enjoys every benefit of the system with none of its costs,
            so selection favours it strongly. It accumulates, the toxin is stripped out,
            and the drive collapses. Watching the rescue allele frequency run away from
            the toxin allele frequency in the results is the clearest possible
            demonstration of why tight linkage between the two halves is not a
            construction detail but the whole basis of the design.
        </p>

        <h3>Base Migration Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation probability (0&ndash;0.5) that an individual moves between
            adjacent populations, decaying tenfold at each step: Pop 1&harr;2 gets the
            base rate, Pop 2&harr;3 base/10, Pop 3&harr;4 base/100, Pop 4&harr;5
            base/1000. Because MEDEA is threshold-dependent when it has any fitness cost,
            migration cuts both ways: it carries the element outwards, but it also
            continually dilutes it with wild-type immigrants, which can hold a population
            below the threshold and keep the element from establishing there. A high
            migration rate can prevent a release from taking hold anywhere.
        </p>

        <h2>What to Look For in the Results</h2>
        <p>
            The <strong>toxin</strong> and <strong>rescue</strong> allele frequency panels
            are the heart of this model. With the two loci fully linked they lie exactly on
            top of each other, because they are physically the same element. Separate them
            and the two curves diverge, with rescue climbing while toxin falls &mdash; the
            signature of recombinational uncoupling.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <strong>adults</strong> panel should stay close to the carrying capacity
            throughout, which is the point: this is modification, not suppression. The
            <strong>eggs</strong> panel is where the element's load shows up, dipping while
            it spreads (many carrier mothers, many non-carrier embryos dying) and then
            recovering once it is near fixation, because by then almost every embryo
            inherits a rescue copy and the killing stops.
        </p>
        <p>
            One further behaviour worth noticing: the wild-type allele never disappears
            completely. Once the element is common, nearly every embryo inherits a rescue
            copy from at least one parent, so residual wild-type alleles ride along
            harmlessly in heterozygotes. Only embryos that inherit no copy from either
            parent die, and those become vanishingly rare. MEDEA reaches fixation as a
            <em>genotype</em> long before it removes the last wild-type allele.
        </p>

        <h2>Fixed Model Assumptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Population carrying capacity:</strong> 500 individuals per population. Egg production above this cap is culled by density-dependent regulation (random selection of survivors).</li>
            <li><strong>Number of populations:</strong> 5, arranged in a linear chain.</li>
            <li><strong>Migration topology:</strong> Linear chain only (1&harr;2&harr;3&harr;4&harr;5), decaying 10&times; per step.</li>
            <li><strong>Replicates:</strong> 3 independent iterations per simulation run, each starting from a fresh wild-type population.</li>
            <li><strong>Release timing:</strong> MEDEA males are released once, at generation 3, into Population 1 only.</li>
            <li><strong>Element location:</strong> Toxin (MTOX) and rescue (MRES) both sit on autosome 2, separated by the configured map distance.</li>
            <li><strong>Toxin is a strict maternal effect:</strong> Only mothers load toxin, and they load it into every egg regardless of that egg's genotype. Fathers contribute nothing.</li>
            <li><strong>Rescue is strictly zygotic:</strong> Immunity comes from the embryo's own rescue copy, inherited from either parent. A rescue copy inherited from the father protects just as well as one from the mother.</li>
            <li><strong>Dominance:</strong> One copy of the toxin is enough to make a mother fully toxic; one copy of the rescue is enough to fully protect an embryo. Homozygotes get no extra dose.</li>
            <li><strong>No nuclease:</strong> MEDEA carries no Cas9 and no guide RNA, so no cutting, homing or resistance-allele formation occurs anywhere in this model. The only genetic change over time is recombination.</li>
            <li><strong>No cargo modelled:</strong> The element carries no additional gene. In a real application the point of the sweep would be to spread a linked cargo, which would add its own fitness cost.</li>
            <li><strong>No parental fitness cost:</strong> Adults carrying the element mate and reproduce normally; the entire load is paid by embryos.</li>
            <li><strong>Non-overlapping generations:</strong> All adults die after reproducing.</li>
            <li><strong>Mating system:</strong> Random mating. Each female mates once per generation; males can mate with many females.</li>
        </ul>

    </div>

</body>
</html>
